# rig egress proxy destination deny-list, generated by `rig webtools setup`.
# The engine is the build's default, fnmatch globs: measured 2026-10-26, BRE
# alternation \(..|..) does NOT match while glob '*' and classes [0-9] do, and
# adding a FilterType line can silently turn all filtering off. Matched against
# the FULL request URI ("FilterURLs On" verified: a deny pattern that appears only
# in a query string still blocks the request), so every rule starts with "://".
# Blacklist semantics: FilterDefaultDeny stays No (Yes would make this a whitelist).
#
# Costs, deliberate: fnmatch '.' matches any character, so a first-label rule
# over-blocks synthetic hostnames — http://10.9x.example.com and
# http://10.abc.example.com are BOTH refused, because the rule anchors on the
# prefix, not on the octet boundary. Over-blocking is loud (403); under-blocking is
# silent, so the globs stay broad on purpose. Anchoring works: 210.abc.example.com
# passes the filter and fails on resolution instead.
#
# Known limits, measured: matching is string-level, so DNS rebinding (a public
# hostname resolving to an internal address) slips through, and a CONNECT tunnel
# can never match a rule (tunnel request lines start with "CONNECT", never "://").
# On Linux, ../firewall closes both at connect time: it drops by the address the
# connection goes to, whatever the name resolved to.
#
# Internal destinations rig must never fetch: loopback, RFC1918, link-local
# including the metadata address, Docker Desktop host-service names,
# docker-internal hostnames, and the cloud metadata host.
://127.*
://10.*
://192.168.*
://169.254.*
://172.1[6-9].*
://172.2[0-9].*
://172.3[0-1].*
://localhost:*
://localhost/*
://*docker.internal*
://metadata.google.internal[:/]*
